{"step_index":307,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-26T06:36:53Z","thinking":"**Investigating 404 Error**\n\nI've hit a snag. Vercel's giving me a standard 404, not the custom one I expected. The code, ID, and all that check out. I'm wondering why Vercel is displaying its default error instead of the one I set up. My next step is to examine the `vercel.json` file closely to figure out what's going on with the route configuration and see if I can find the source of this problem.\n\n\n**Analyzing Route Configuration**\n\nI've been examining the `vercel.json` file. It seems the issue might stem from how Vercel handles static files with a Python runtime. I'm focusing on discrepancies in routing with the \"public/\" folder, and wondering if this is the cause. I'm also comparing this to how Vercel Python deployments for FastAPI work seamlessly without ANY build errors, 404s, or crashes. I'm going to consult Vercel's FastAPI template for the answer.\n\n\n**Simplifying Static File Routing**\n\nI've been examining the optimal `vercel.json` configuration and have a simple, fail-proof approach. It seems Vercel's zero-config setup for FastAPI is key! If I put the entry point in `api/index.py`, Vercel automatically routes everything seamlessly, even static files in the public directory! This approach seems to bypass the earlier issues completely, resolving many points that were concerning me.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"find_by_name","args":{"Pattern":"index.py","SearchDirectory":"C:\\Users\\dell\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\228fe0bc-0a77-489e-a1ba-57d617247457","toolAction":"Checking index.py","toolSummary":"Checking index.py"}}]}
